# minimum qore version
%requires qore >= 2.0

# don't use "$" for vars, members, and methods, assume local variable scope
%modern

%requires(reexport) DataProvider
%requires(reexport) RestSchemaDataProvider
%requires FileLocationHandler

%requires json
%requires yaml

module RestSchemaActions {
    version = "1.0";
    desc = "user module for registering data provider app actions from REST schemas";
    author = "Qore Technologies <info@qoretechnologies.com>";
    url = "https://qore.org";
    license = "MIT";
}

/** @mainpage RestSchemaActions Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section restschemaactionsintro RestSchemaActions Introduction

    The %RestSchemaActions module provides the reusable machinery for turning an explicitly selected
    set of operations in a REST schema into data provider application actions.

    It exists because a schema describes a **transport contract**, and an application action is a
    **product decision**.  A schema says which parameters an operation takes; it does not say which
    of them a user should see first, which identifiers should be selectable rather than typed by
    hand, how actions should be grouped, or which wire-level integer is really a date.  Generating
    actions straight from a schema therefore produces a technically correct action with an unusable
    form, while hand-writing them duplicates the whole request and response contract in %Qore and
    leaves every upstream change to be found and reproduced by hand.

    This module assigns each concern to whichever source is best placed to maintain it:

    |!Concern|!Owner
    |Paths, methods, parameters, bodies, requiredness, enums and references|the pinned upstream schema
    |HTTP execution and wire serialization|@ref restschemadataproviderintro "RestSchemaDataProvider"
    |Which operations are exported, and their stable action identity|an application-owned manifest
    |Names, ordering, grouping, reference data and semantic types|an application-owned UX overlay

    @section restschemaactions_pruning Schema Normalization and Pruning

    @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaPruner "RestSchemaPruner" reduces a schema to the operations an
    application selected, together with the transitive closure of everything they reference.  This
    is what keeps a large upstream document - Stripe's declares several hundred paths - from
    dominating load time and memory when an application exposes a couple of dozen of them.

    Pruning is a **structural** transformation: it never edits a schema to work around an upstream
    defect or a construct %Qore does not yet support.  An invalid document belongs in a
    normalization step of its own, and a valid construct that fails to parse belongs in
    @ref openapi3intro "OpenApi3".

    @section restschemaactions_manifest The Operation Manifest

    An application declares what it exports as a list of
    @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaActionInfo "RestSchemaActionInfo" entries, and hands them to
    @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaActionSet "RestSchemaActionSet" together with the schema.

    Each entry carries a **stable action ID**.  An OpenAPI \c operationId may be recorded, and is
    checked, but it is never the source of action identity: an upstream rename has to be reported as
    drift rather than silently rename an action that existing workflows call.  The manifest also makes
    additions deliberate - an endpoint appearing in an updated schema does not become a public action
    until somebody adds it.

    Everything is validated when the set is constructed, so a manifest that has drifted from its
    schema fails when the module loads rather than when a user opens the action.

    @section restschemaactions_flattening Request Flattening

    A schema-derived REST request groups values by where they travel, and path variables do not appear
    in the request type at all.  None of that means anything to somebody filling in a form, so
    @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaActionOperation "RestSchemaActionOperation" lifts every value to
    one flat level and keeps a reverse map, so the exact transport request can be rebuilt:

    |!Location|!Where the value travels
    |@ref RestSchemaActions::RSAL_PATH "path"|substituted into the URI path template
    |@ref RestSchemaActions::RSAL_QUERY "query"|a URI query argument
    |@ref RestSchemaActions::RSAL_HEADER "header"|an HTTP header
    |@ref RestSchemaActions::RSAL_BODY "body"|a property of an object-valued request body
    |@ref RestSchemaActions::RSAL_BODY_ROOT "body-root"|the whole request body, when it is not an object

    Flattening removes transport groupings and nothing else: a body property that is itself an object
    stays an object.  When two locations produce the same name the collision is **reported**, not
    resolved by picking one; the manifest renames either side with a location-qualified overlay key.

    The reverse mapping is part of the contract, so
    @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaActionOperation::flatten() "flatten()" is provided as the exact
    inverse of @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaActionOperation::unflatten() "unflatten()" and is
    round-trip tested.

    @section restschemaactions_overlay The UX Overlay

    @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaFieldOverlayInfo "RestSchemaFieldOverlayInfo" declares
    presentation: display names and descriptions, field order and preselection, static allowed values,
    \c required_groups, dependencies, semantic types, and fields to ignore because the connection owns
    them.  It is applied before action options are generated, so every override reaches
    @ref DataProvider::DataProviderActionCatalog::getActionOptionFromFields() "getActionOptionFromFields()".

    Requiredness may only be **narrowed**.  An action may present a stricter contract than the API;
    declaring a schema-required value optional would produce requests the server rejects, so it is an
    error.

    An overlay must never be used for structural repair.  A nicer label belongs here; a malformed
    reference belongs in normalization; a valid construct %Qore cannot parse belongs in
    @ref openapi3intro "OpenApi3".

    @section restschemaactions_codecs Wire Codecs

    A schema can say that a value is an integer; it cannot say the integer is a point in time, still
    less whether the unit is seconds or milliseconds.  A field overlay names a codec from
    @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaActionCodecs "RestSchemaActionCodecs", and the field is then
    published with the codec's type and converted in both directions - so the manifest stays
    declarative and the unit is decided once, in one place, instead of by every caller.

    @section restschemaactions_refdata Reference Data

    An identifier presented as a free-text field is one of the clearest signs of an action generated
    straight from a schema.  @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaReferenceDataInfo "RestSchemaReferenceDataInfo"
    turns one into a dropdown by naming another action in the same set and the record fields supplying
    the value and the label; no application code is involved.  Dropdowns are then served through
    @ref DataProvider::AbstractDataProvider::getSupportedReferenceData() "getSupportedReferenceData()"
    and @ref DataProvider::AbstractDataProvider::getReferenceData() "getReferenceData()".

    Most identifiers are not global.  A branch belongs to a project, a page to a space, a variable to a
    group - so listing them requires the value the user has already chosen elsewhere in the same form.
    @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaReferenceDataInfo::options_from "options_from" declares that:
    it forwards named options of the action being filled in into the listing action, and the dropdown
    stays empty until they have values, rather than offering identifiers from some other scope.

    @section restschemaactions_import Importing a Schema

    @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaImporter "RestSchemaImporter" produces the committed artifact:
    download a named upstream revision, prune it to the manifest's operations, write it, and update the
    application's provenance record.  \c tools/rest-schema-import.qr is its command-line front end and
    also runs the drift report below, so that an update is one reproducible command rather than an ad-hoc
    program written once per application.

    It normalizes a trailing slash and a path-parameter **name** when resolving a manifest entry against a
    document, because neither changes the operation, and comparing paths as strings therefore reports
    operations as withdrawn when they are merely spelled differently.  Every match that needed normalizing
    is reported and fails the import: the manifest still has to name the path exactly as the document
    declares it, or @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaActionSet "RestSchemaActionSet" will not resolve it
    at run time.

    @section restschemaactions_execution Execution

    @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaActionSetDataProvider "RestSchemaActionSetDataProvider" resolves
    each action ID to a @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaActionDataProvider "RestSchemaActionDataProvider",
    which rebuilds the transport request and executes it through
    @ref restschemadataproviderintro "RestSchemaDataProvider".

    The action publishes the **success response body** as its output type, not the HTTP envelope, so
    downstream users are not made to navigate a wrapper merely because execution also has a status and
    headers.  When an operation declares more than one success response with a body, no output
    contract is guessed: registration fails until the manifest selects one.

    @note Actions can currently only be generated from OpenAPI 3 schemas; a Swagger 2.0 schema is
    rejected with a clear error rather than handled by untested code paths.

    @section restschemaactions_records Record-Based Tables

    An action is a request; a **record table** is a collection a caller can search, create in, update and
    delete from with the standard record API, and with the standard search expressions the rest of the
    platform speaks.  A REST collection can be presented as one, and
    @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaTableDataProvider "RestSchemaTableDataProvider" does it without a
    second implementation of the API: a table is declared as a
    @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaRecordTableInfo "RestSchemaRecordTableInfo" naming **actions the
    manifest already exports**, so the paths, the request contracts, the escaping, the codecs and the
    published response shapes stay in one place.

    @subsection restschemaactions_records_hybrid Hybrid Pushdown

    A REST collection is not a table.  The service evaluates a fixed set of filters, expressed as query
    arguments or - in the two cases where a vendor publishes a query language - as a filter expression, and
    it evaluates nothing else.  Answering a search therefore takes three things at once, and doing any of
    them alone is wrong:

    |!Step|!Why
    |Send what the service can evaluate|a collection of ten thousand records behind a page of 100 cannot be searched by reading all of it
    |Evaluate the rest as records arrive|the answer has to be the one the caller asked for, not the service's approximation of it
    |Keep reading pages until the limit is met by **matching** records|otherwise a search silently under-returns

    @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaRecordFilter "RestSchemaRecordFilter" performs the split, and the third
    step falls out of applying the residual predicate in
    @ref DataProvider::DefaultRecordIterator "DefaultRecordIterator" - which counts the limit **after**
    filtering - over a lazy @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaRecordPageIterator "RestSchemaRecordPageIterator".
    Truncating the fetch at the limit and filtering afterwards is the way to get this wrong: asked for ten
    closed issues from a repository holding ten closed among a hundred, it answers with one.

    @subsection restschemaactions_records_exact Exactness Is the Load-Bearing Property

    Each filter rule says whether the service's filter means **exactly** what the operator means.  It
    defaults to @ref False, which is the answer that cannot produce a wrong result: the filter is sent and
    the predicate is evaluated again on what comes back, so a service that returns more than it was asked
    for costs a little client-side work and nothing else.  Declaring a rule exact is a statement about the
    API, and it is the one way this machinery can under-return.

    @subsection restschemaactions_records_native What Native Search Means

    @ref DataProvider::DataProviderInfo::supports_native_search "supports_native_search" is declared only by
    a table whose API takes a filter **expression** and can therefore evaluate a whole tree.  A table that
    can only narrow a listing with query arguments declares it @ref False however many arguments it has,
    because a caller reading that flag is asking whether the search happens on the server, and for such a
    table the answer is "partly".  The per-operator detail is published as
    @ref DataProvider::DataProviderExpressionInfo::supports_pushdown "supports_pushdown", and every generic
    search operator is answered whether the service can evaluate it or not.

    @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaQuerySyntaxInfo "RestSchemaQuerySyntaxInfo" declares such a language
    without any application code: a token per operator in one of three rendering forms - infix
    <tt>field op value</tt>, prefix <tt>op(field,value)</tt> or method <tt>field.Method(value)</tt> - and one
    function rendering a value.  An operator the language spells without quite meaning it is listed in
    \c inexact_ops, so it narrows the listing and the predicate is still answered here.

    @subsection restschemaactions_records_smaller A Language Is Usually Smaller Than the Renderer Assumes

    No vendor in this family publishes a complete boolean algebra, and assuming one is how a search comes back
    wrong rather than empty:

    |!What varies|!How it is declared|!What it prevents
    |A language with no OR or no NOT|leave \c or_op or \c not_op unset|the connective cannot be rendered, so the expression stays whole and is evaluated client-side.  Klaviyo's \c filter has neither, and sending the arguments of an OR joined by anything else would answer with records the caller excluded
    |A language with no grouping|\c and_sep / \c or_sep give the literal separator, \c parenthesize says whether a group is wrapped|Klaviyo joins predicates with a bare comma and rejects a parenthesised one
    |A query that is not a string|\c render_query renders the whole expression and may return any type|Intercom's search takes a **tree of hashes** in the request body

    The split matters: the operator tables still decide **what is pushed down** even when \c render_query
    decides **how it is spelled**, so
    @ref DataProvider::DataProviderInfo::supports_native_search "supports_native_search",
    @ref DataProvider::DataProviderExpressionInfo::supports_pushdown "supports_pushdown" and the residual
    predicate all stay declarative.  A hook that also decided pushdown would put the honesty of those flags
    inside a closure.

    @subsection restschemaactions_records_raw A Rendered Query Is Already in Wire Form

    The argument a table renders its query into is often the same argument an **action** publishes in a
    curated form.  Klaviyo's \c filter takes a whole filter expression, and the listing actions put a wire
    codec on it that publishes the four named fields the application they replace offered.

    A rendered query must not be converted by that codec: it is already exactly what the wire takes, and
    Klaviyo's codec answers a shape it does not recognise with @ref nothing - which would drop the filter
    and answer the search with the **whole collection**, with no error anywhere.  So the query value is
    placed in a @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaRawValue "RestSchemaRawValue", which
    @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaActionOperation::unflatten() "unflatten()" places unchanged.  The
    option is still one the action has to declare and is checked as any other is; only the conversion is
    skipped.  This is what lets a table reuse the listing action the manifest already exports instead of
    declaring a second copy of the same path with the codec left off.

    @subsection restschemaactions_records_type What the Columns Are

    A listing returns a **projection**; reading one record returns the **record**.  A vendor describes both
    and the two rarely agree - GitLab describes a project with 24 fields in its listing and 161 in its single
    read - so the record type is derived from both and the richer one is published, with any column only the
    other declares added to it rather than lost.

    That is not a cosmetic difference.  A column the table does not declare **cannot be searched at all**:
    the framework rejects a predicate on an undeclared field before the table is reached.  Under-declaring
    therefore removes functionality silently, which is why the richer description wins and why the two are
    merged rather than one being chosen.

    Where a vendor returns a field and describes it nowhere -
    @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaRecordTableInfo::extra_columns "extra_columns" declares it, merged over
    the derived type.  That keeps the schema the primary description and leaves a short list of what it is
    missing, rather than a hand-written record type that goes stale the moment the vendor adds a field.
    Bitbucket is the case: it addresses a repository by a \c slug it returns in every response and declares
    in no schema, and without that column the table could not address a record at all.

    @subsection restschemaactions_records_paths The Paths a Record Is Read Through

    @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaRecordTableInfo::records_path "records_path",
    @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaRecordTableInfo::record_path "record_path" and
    @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaRecordTableInfo::write_record_path "write_record_path" say where in a
    response the records are, and a path that does not resolve fails **silently**: a single read whose record
    path names a wrapper the action has already unwrapped reads one record as nothing at all, and no test
    that only searches will ever notice.

    @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaTablesDataProvider::checkTables() "checkTables()" therefore resolves
    every one of them against the response the action publishes, which is what an application's test suite
    calls.  Only a response that describes its fields can be checked; a union describes none, and an
    unverifiable path is left alone rather than reported on a guess.

    @subsection restschemaactions_records_identity Addressing One Record

    A table addresses a record by whatever the API's paths take, which is not always one value and is not
    always a top-level field.  @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaRecordTableInfo::id_parts "id_parts"
    declares a composite identity as option to record field path - GitHub names a repository by its
    \c {owner} and its \c {repo}, and carries the first as a nested \c "owner.login".

    That is a different thing from
    @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaRecordTableInfo::scope_options "scope_options", and the difference is
    what decides between them:

    |!|!Identifies|!Comes from
    |a scope option|the **collection**, the same for every record in a search|the caller, and the listing requires it
    |an identity part|**one record**, varying from row to row within a listing|the record itself

    A search whose predicate is the identity and nothing else is answered by reading that one record.  A
    predicate that also asks something more is not, because answering only the identity half would return -
    or write to - a record the caller did not match.

    @subsection restschemaactions_records_actions A Table Is Reached by an Action

    Declaring the providers is only half of it: a table no action reaches is offered to nobody.
    @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaTablesDataProvider::registerRecordActions() "registerRecordActions()"
    registers the record-based actions against \c "<container>/{table}", and the IDs are the application's to
    choose - where a native application replaces an existing one, they are the IDs the superseded application
    registered.

    @section restschemaactions_drift Schema Updates

    Staying aligned with an API has to mean a **reproducible reviewed update**, not an uncontrolled
    runtime one - a schema fetched at run time could change the contract of an action that existing
    workflows already call.

    @ref RestSchemaActions::RestSchemaDrift "RestSchemaDrift" produces the report that makes such a
    review possible.  Given the action set an application ships and a candidate schema, it says exactly
    what would change about every exported action, and classifies each difference as one that can be
    adopted or one that needs a decision:

    |!Adoptable|!Needs review
    |a new option|an option removed, moved, or changed type
    |a new operation on an exported path|an operation removed or its \c operationId renamed
    |a documentation change|an option becoming required, or losing an allowed value
    ||the request content type or the published response changing

    An update is then: import a named revision, run the report, adopt it or stop.

    @section restschemaactionsrelnotes Release Notes

    @subsection restschemaactionsv1_0 RestSchemaActions v1.0
    - the initial version of the %RestSchemaActions module
*/

public namespace RestSchemaActions {
}
